Upgrading NicTool
=================

These steps apply to upgrades between modern NicTool releases (2.10 and
later). If you are upgrading from a pre-2.10 install, read the appendix
at the end of this document first.

0. Take a backup

    # mysqldump -u root -p nictool | gzip > nictool-$(date +%F).sql.gz

   There is no sql/downgrade script. The database migration alters
   tables in place. Do not skip this step.

1. Stop the export processes (stops DNS server updates)

   daemontools:
    # svc -d /service/ns1.example.com   (repeat per nameserver)
   or cron: comment out the nt_export.pl crontab entries.

2. Stop the web server (prevents DB changes during the migration)

    # systemctl stop apache2      (Debian/Ubuntu)
    # service httpd stop          (RHEL-ish)
    # /usr/local/etc/rc.d/apache24 stop   (FreeBSD)

3. Install the new NicToolServer and NicToolClient

   https://github.com/NicTool/NicTool/wiki/Install-NicToolServer
   https://github.com/NicTool/NicTool/wiki/Install-NicToolClient

   Then install any new perl dependencies:

    # cd /usr/local/nictool/server && perl bin/nt_install_deps.pl

4. Run the database migration

    # cd /usr/local/nictool/server
    # sql/upgrade.pl

   Notes:

   * The script detects which updates your database still needs, and is
     safe to re-run: already-applied steps are skipped.

   * Upgrades crossing 2.41 convert all tables to InnoDB and add foreign
     key constraints. If audit-log rows reference parent rows that no
     longer exist, the script prints the affected rows and stops with
     instructions. Nothing is deleted without your say-so (--prune).

   * Upgrades crossing 2.35 convert the database from utf8/latin1 to
     utf8mb4. If your database predates utf8 defaults AND holds
     non-ASCII data (names, descriptions, contact addresses), be aware
     that a charset conversion re-encodes bytes according to each
     column's declared charset: data that was stored through a
     mismatched connection charset (mojibake) can be garbled further.
     Verify any non-ASCII rows on a restored copy of your backup before
     upgrading production. Pure-ASCII data (the common case for DNS) is
     unaffected.

5. Start the web server and validate

    # systemctl start apache2

   Log in, browse a few zones, edit and revert a record.

6. Stage an export and compare it -- with exports still stopped

   Do not resume the export service yet. The generated run file invokes
   nt_export.pl with -force -pfextra, which publishes immediately: the
   BIND postflight compiles, rsyncs, and restarts the nameserver, and
   the tinydns postflight compiles and rsyncs data.cdb into place. Stage
   an export by hand and compare it against the pre-upgrade output
   before any of that happens.

   First save the last known-good export output:

    # cd /usr/local/nictool/ns1.example.com
    # cp -a data data.pre-upgrade      (tinydns)
    # cp -a data-ns1.example.com data.pre-upgrade   (BIND: the zone-file dir)

   tinydns only: the export postflight always runs 'make remote', so
   temporarily disable publishing by removing the rsync line under the
   'remote:' target in ./Makefile (an empty recipe is fine; restore it
   in step 7).

   Then stage a fresh export. Omitting -pfextra keeps the BIND
   postflight from compiling, rsyncing, or restarting anything:

    # ./nt_export.pl -nsid N -force

   Compare the staged output against the saved copy -- for tinydns sort
   both data files first, then diff; for BIND, diff -r the two
   directories. Expect only formatting differences, e.g. as of 2.41:
   quotes inside TXT records are stripped, and SOA records with
   all-default timers export empty fields instead of 0:0:0:0. Record
   data itself should not change. If it does, stop and investigate
   before publishing.

7. Publish, then resume exports

   The staged export in step 6 was recorded as a successful export even
   though nothing was published, so a later non-forced export (the cron
   model) can see "no changes" and skip publishing. Don't rely on the
   schedule for the first publish: restore the tinydns Makefile
   'remote:' target if you disabled it, then run one forced publishing
   export by hand and check that it succeeds:

    # ./nt_export.pl -nsid N -force -pfextra

   Once that completes cleanly, resume the normal service:

    # svc -u /service/ns1.example.com

   or re-enable the nt_export.pl crontab entries.


Appendix: upgrading from pre-2.10
=================================

The export system was rewritten in 2.10. Each nameserver gets a
directory containing a 'run' file and a Makefile that handles any
compilation and rsyncing:

    # cd /usr/local/nictool
    # mkdir ns1.example.com && cd ns1.example.com
    # ln -s ../server/bin/nt_export.pl .
    # ./nt_export.pl -nsid N     (run without -nsid to see the list)
    # ./run                      (then follow the instructions in it)
    # ln -s /usr/local/nictool/ns1.example.com /service/

See https://github.com/NicTool/NicTool/wiki/Export for details.

Differences from the pre-2.10 exports worth knowing:

 * Zones with no resource records now publish SOA records.
 * SRV records export as generic tinydns records (no tinydns patch
   needed).
 * The pure-perl exporter is somewhat slower than the old C++ dump on
   very large installs (a full export of ~150k zones / 1.2M RRs takes
   on the order of a minute); incremental exports are the norm and are
   fast. Test an export against a snapshot of your data if in doubt.
